// THE BOOK OF GATES, in Budge's English from The Egyptian Heaven and Hell,
// vol. II (1905), division by division.
//
// The text comes from book-of-gates-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/budge-underworld.py. Budge numbers twelve divisions of the
// night: the first is the western ante-chamber, before any gate; the second
// to twelfth each open with a gate (his first to eleventh) guarded by an
// upright serpent. Each division is a passage with no text of its own;
// beneath it come the gate, the boat of the sun in the middle register, and
// the registers on either side. Budge's chapters VII and VIII (the Judgment
// Hall of Osiris and the rest of the sixth division) are one division here;
// for its registers he gives first the damaged scenes of Seti I's sarcophagus
// and then the complete ones from the tomb of Ramesses VI, and both are kept.
//
// Budge follows the alabaster sarcophagus of Seti I. His divisions are not
// aligned with Hornung's hours or scenes, which count the first and last
// hours differently. Hornung and the modern English translations are cited,
// never quoted. Nothing is marked verified, and inventoryVerified stays false.

